  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Phase 2 of the Middle East Humanitarian Appeal (Emergency Response in Gaza and Lebanon)</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Following an intense war with Israel, Lebanon is still in the midst of a crisis where ongoing military activities and the widespread destruction of homes, infrastructure, and essential services&#x2014;both during the conflict and after the cessation of hostilities&#x2014;continue to hinder the safe return of displaced individuals. These challenges also restrict access to humanitarian aid, recovery efforts, and protection services, leaving affected populations in prolonged vulnerability.The vulnerable returnees, remainees, internally displaced persons (IDPs), and secondary displaced communities continue to struggle to make ends meet due to devastated livelihoods and a lack of sustainable income sources.By providing basic and essential necessities like food and hygiene, the Phase II project seeks to meet the immediate needs of 1,800 HHs (9,000 individuals)  that are at risk. This will enable them to save money on other necessary expenses and restore a sense of normalcy to their lives.In order to address the nutritional needs of the family, especially those who are children, elderly, or disabled, the project designed a food parcel that includes the dietary requirements of every family member and is based on culturally acceptable contents.Similarly, hygiene kits have been carefully designed according to the needs of the communities, recommendations from the sector, and enable households to maintain proper hygiene practices and ensure the health and well-being of the family. Along with hygiene kits, the project will design and disseminate IEC (Information, Educational, and Communication) material and arrange hygiene awareness sessions to promote good hygiene practices.It&apos;s worth mentioning that all the interventions will be arranged in close coordination with the sectors (Food Security and WASH) to ensure that the project bridges the gap and avoide any possible duplication of assistance. In addition, the project will continue to coordinate with the DRR focal points to ensure that the assistance reaches the most vulnerable groups.</narrative>
  </description>
